---
title: 'épisode 13 : petit bobo'
tags:
  - fr
  - language
  - learning
---

Q: Say “the head” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La tête.`}}
N: CD2, piste 22, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the arm” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le bras.`}}
N: Masculine. `Bras` has the same spelling in the singular and plural. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the leg” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La jambe.`}}
N: CD2, piste 22, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the hand” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La main.`}}
N: Feminine despite its ending. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the foot” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le pied.`}}
N: Singular `le pied`; plural `les pieds`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the fingers” in French.
A: {{`Les doigts.`}}
N: Singular `un doigt`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the toes” in French.
A: {{`Les orteils.`}}
N: Singular `un orteil`. Fingers are `les doigts`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the elbow” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le coude.`}}
N: CD2, piste 22, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the knee” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le genou.`}}
N: Plural `les genoux`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the back” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le dos.`}}
N: `Dos` has the same spelling in the singular and plural. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the neck” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le cou.`}}
N: `Le cou` is the neck; `le coude` is the elbow. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “a shoulder” in French so its gender is visible.
A: {{`Une épaule.`}}
N: Definite form `l'épaule`; plural `les épaules`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the buttocks” in French.
A: {{`Les fesses.`}}
N: Singular `une fesse`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the belly” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le ventre.`}}
N: Also abdomen in this body-part context. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the chest” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La poitrine.`}}
N: CD2, piste 22, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the tongue” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La langue.`}}
N: `Langue` means tongue in the body-part exercise, language in `la langue française`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “an ear” in French so its gender is visible.
A: {{`Une oreille.`}}
N: Definite form `l'oreille`; plural `les oreilles`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the nose” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le nez.`}}
N: CD2, piste 23, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the mouth” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La bouche.`}}
N: CD2, piste 23, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the eyes” in French.
A: {{`Les yeux.`}}
N: Singular `un œil`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the teeth” in French.
A: {{`Les dents.`}}
N: Singular `une dent`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “the throat” in French, with its definite article.
A: {{`La gorge.`}}
N: CD2, piste 23, manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What does `un bobo` mean in episode 13?
A: A minor hurt or health problem.
N: Familiar vocabulary in the opening activity, manuel p. 96. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “I have a cold” using the wording in episode 13's answer key.
A: {{`J'ai le rhume.`}}
N: `J'ai un rhume` is also possible. Manuel p. 96, corrigé p. 152. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “I have a fever” with the expression taught in episode 13.
A: {{`Je fais de la fièvre.`}}
N: `Faire de la fièvre`, CD2 piste 21, transcript p. 138. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What does `avoir mal au cœur` mean in the symptom exercise?
A: To feel nauseous or sick to one's stomach.
N: Manuel p. 98, exercise 5, image c. A vocabulary expression, not a diagnosis. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: In the phone dialogue, what does Québec French `C'est plate` express?
A: “That's a shame” or disappointment about the situation.
N: Maryse reacts to Hélène being unable to attend her birthday celebration. Transcript p. 138. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: J'[ai|avoir, présent] mal à la tête.
N: `Avoir mal`, manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Tu [as|avoir, présent] mal au dos.
N: `Avoir mal`, manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Elle [a|avoir, présent] mal à la gorge.
N: `Il`, `elle` and `on` share the form `a`. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Nous [avons|avoir, présent] mal aux pieds.
N: `Avoir mal`, manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Vous [avez|avoir, présent] mal aux dents.
N: `Avoir mal`, manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Elles [ont|avoir, présent] mal à la tête.
N: The verb agrees with the people feeling pain. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Il a mal [au|à + le] ventre.
N: Masculine singular noun. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Elle a mal [à la|feminine singular] main.
N: `La main` keeps its article after `à`. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: J'ai mal [à l'|singular before a vowel]épaule.
N: `Une épaule` is feminine; the definite article is elided. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

C: Tu as mal [aux|à + les] yeux.
N: Plural takes `aux` even before a vowel. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What is the French plural of `un genou`?
A: {{`Les genoux.`}}
N: Irregular plural used in cahier p. 81. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What is the French singular of `les yeux`?
A: {{`Un œil.`}}
N: Masculine; `j'ai mal à l'œil`. Manuel p. 97. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “I don't feel well” using `se sentir`.
A: {{`Je ne me sens pas bien.`}}
N: Keep `me`; negation surrounds the pronoun-verb group. CD2 piste 21. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “I'm not doing very well” using `aller`.
A: {{`Je ne vais pas très bien.`}}
N: One of Hélène's two expressions in exercise 1c, manuel p. 96. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Ask informally “Where does it hurt?” using `est-ce que`.
A: {{`Où est-ce que tu as mal ?`}}
N: An open question asking for a body part. Added practice from `avoir mal`. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Ask “How do you feel?” using `tu` and `est-ce que`.
A: {{`Comment est-ce que tu te sens ?`}}
N: `Tu te sens`, with the reflexive pronoun. Added practice. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Ask “Do you have a headache?” using `tu` and `est-ce que`.
A: {{`Est-ce que tu as mal à la tête ?`}}
N: A closed yes/no question. Added practice. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Ask “Do your teeth hurt?” using `tu` and inversion.
A: {{`As-tu mal aux dents ?`}}
N: Join verb and subject with a hyphen; `à + les = aux`. Added practice. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “Get some good rest!” to someone you address as `tu`.
A: {{`Repose-toi bien !`}}
N: Imperative of `se reposer`, with `toi` after the verb. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “Take care of yourself!” with `prendre soin` and `tu`.
A: {{`Prends soin de toi !`}}
N: `Prendre soin de quelqu'un`. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “Poor you!” in French.
A: {{`Pauvre toi !`}}
N: Maryse uses this to express sympathy. Manuel p. 98 and transcript p. 138. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Give the affectionate “Poor sweetheart!” expression from episode 13.
A: {{`Pauvre petit chou !`}}
N: The manual labels this familiar and affectionate, p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Say “Take good care of yourself!” using `se soigner` and `tu`.
A: {{`Soigne-toi bien !`}}
N: The regular `-er` imperative has no final `s` here. Manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Change `Fais attention à toi !` to the `vous` form.
A: {{`Faites attention à vous !`}}
N: Imperative of `faire`: `fais` with `tu`, `faites` with `vous`. Practice based on manuel p. 98. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What birthday wish does Hélène write to Maryse in Québec French?
A: {{`Bonne fête, Maryse !`}}
N: Birthday context, manuel p. 99. `Bonne année` concerns the new year. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Give the formal “Get well soon!” wish from the card exercise.
A: {{`Prompt rétablissement !`}}
N: CD2 piste 25, wish a. Manuel p. 99. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Give the wedding wish from CD2 piste 25.
A: {{`Meilleurs vœux pour votre mariage !`}}
N: Wish b, manuel p. 99. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Give the sympathy message used after a death in episode 13.
A: {{`Sincères condoléances.`}}
N: The printed wish uses a period. CD2 piste 25, wish c. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What French wish fits someone who is retiring?
A: {{`Bonne retraite !`}}
N: CD2 piste 25, wish d. Manuel p. 99. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: Congratulate someone on their new baby using the CD2 piste 25 wording.
A: {{`Félicitations pour votre bébé !`}}
N: Wish e, manuel p. 99. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What does `app.` stand for in the envelope exercise?
A: {{`Appartement.`}}
N: Manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What does `av.` stand for in a French address?
A: {{`Avenue.`}}
N: Manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What does `boul.` stand for in a French address?
A: {{`Boulevard.`}}
N: Manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: What does `nº` stand for in the address exercise?
A: {{`Numéro.`}}
N: Manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: In the textbook envelope layout, where does the sender's address go?
A: {{`En haut à gauche.`}}
N: `L'expéditeur` or `l'expéditrice` sends the card. Zone 1, manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: In the textbook envelope layout, where does the recipient's address go?
A: {{`Au centre.`}}
N: `Le destinataire` or `la destinataire` receives the card. Zone 2, manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]

---

Q: In the textbook envelope layout, where does the stamp go?
A: {{`En haut à droite.`}}
N: `Le timbre`, zone 3, manuel p. 101. [[fr/episode-13]]
